(declared leaves-interrupt-hardware-alone yes (clause "after the first enabling of interrupts, application code runs only as a declared task's job or as a service's application code; and no application code, a task's, a service's or application initialisation's, writes the timer's counter or compare, the interrupt controller's configuration, or the hart's interrupt or trap state other than through the runtime API's masking primitives (a job's only: the fault contract, Terms) or reads or writes the controller's claim and complete registers, a read of which claims, or a declared source's device registers; no service calls a runtime primitive; and no application code releases a task or calls a runtime function other than the runtime API record's primitives and its completion path. A runtime function is any function of a catalog record's implementation, a driver's included, called after the first enabling of interrupts; before it, application initialisation reaches that hardware only through catalog functions, which `runtime-discipline.<id>` covers. … Application code calls no entry of the fault path — in initialisation, before the first enabling of interrupts, as in a job or a service's application code after it; a panic reaching the image's handler is no such call … The runtime's own data is what this section's facts read in memory or in a register a port reserves: what the scheduler holds, its mask depth among it, what the timer service holds of the next release, the plan's tables, each saved context, and what a service, a record's or generated, holds of its source. Application code writes none of it, nor any other data or code of a catalog record's implementation or of the code the build generates, but through the calls `leaves-interrupt-hardware-alone` lets it make, or into what a call between it and that code hands it to fill, and it hands no call a place in any of it to write, but what that code handed it; in initialisation as in a job or a service's application code, and by no other means, a stray pointer or an overrun of its own stack bound among them (its own stack, within its bound, is its own, and a write a guard stops writes nothing, and is the fault contract's). For this clause a runtime function is also a function of the code the build generates, but for a node's code its task line's `uses` lists or its own `bind` lines name (the runtime plan's §2.2, `RTP-H5`, `RTP-H13`), which §7 says what charges; and a job's code calls the completion path only as its last act, the path not returning to it.") (basis "it touches no device"))
